    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c5e32481-22b8-5c40-826b-b048c9f9298d",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following is a distinct line parallel to $18x+3y=-12$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $18x+3y=-12$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18x+3y &= -12 \\\\\n3y &= -18x-12 \\\\\ny &= -6x-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the required slope is $-6$.\n\nRewrite the choices in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x-2y=8 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-6x-4 \\\\\n9x+12y=-24 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{3}{4}x-2 \\\\\n-18x-3y=-6 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-6x+2 \\\\\n9x+12y=-36 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{3}{4}x-3 \\\\\n-6x+8y=-24 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac{3}{4}x-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line $-12x-2y=8$ has both the same slope $-6$ and the same $y$-intercept $-4$ as the given line. It is the same line, so it is not a distinct parallel line.\n\nThe line $-18x-3y=-6$ has slope $-6$ and $y$-intercept $2$, different from $-4$. Therefore, it is a distinct parallel line.\n\n$$\n-18x-3y=-6\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2993458e-72e5-5bf5-a534-981bd5a055cf",
