    "question": {
      ":question/id": "df60ad28-2166-52d8-9c2a-5ca277d80ff4",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following is a distinct line parallel to $6x-8y=-40$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $6x-8y=-40$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x-8y &= -40 \\\\\n-8y &= -6x-40 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3}{4}x+5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the required slope is $\\frac{3}{4}$.\n\nRewrite the choices in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x-2y=8 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=2x-4 \\\\\n-6x+3y=-3 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=2x-1 \\\\\n-6x+8y=40 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac{3}{4}x+5 \\\\\n3x-4y=-4 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1 \\\\\n-6x-3y=3 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-2x-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line $-6x+8y=40$ has both the same slope $\\frac{3}{4}$ and the same $y$-intercept $5$ as the given line. It is the same line, so it is not a distinct parallel line.\n\nThe line $3x-4y=-4$ has slope $\\frac{3}{4}$ and $y$-intercept $1$, different from $5$. Therefore, it is a distinct parallel line.\n\n$$\n3x-4y=-4\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
