    "question": {
      ":question/id": "434312c9-3d4d-5ba4-b1b6-228fa0052bd8",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following is a distinct line parallel to $4x-y=-3$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $4x-y=-3$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x-y &= -3 \\\\\n-y &= -4x-3 \\\\\ny &= 4x+3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the required slope is $4$.\n\nRewrite the choices in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x+3y=9 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=4x+3 \\\\\n6x+2y=8 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-3x+4 \\\\\n-12x-9y=18 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{4}{3}x-2 \\\\\n6x+2y=-6 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-3x-3 \\\\\n8x-2y=4 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=4x-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line $-12x+3y=9$ has both the same slope $4$ and the same $y$-intercept $3$ as the given line. It is the same line, so it is not a distinct parallel line.\n\nThe line $8x-2y=4$ has slope $4$ and $y$-intercept $-2$, different from $3$. Therefore, it is a distinct parallel line.\n\n$$\n8x-2y=4\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
