    "question": {
      ":question/id": "373b14f6-27aa-561e-bf1d-018d35ccc0c3",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following is a distinct line parallel to $2x+3y=-15$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $2x+3y=-15$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x+3y &= -15 \\\\\n3y &= -2x-15 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the required slope is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nRewrite the choices in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x+6y=-30 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-5 \\\\\n-4x+6y=-24 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac{2}{3}x-4 \\\\\n-3x-6y=12 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{1}{2}x-2 \\\\\n-4x+6y=24 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\\n-6x-9y=0 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line $4x+6y=-30$ has both the same slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$ and the same $y$-intercept $-5$ as the given line. It is the same line, so it is not a distinct parallel line.\n\nThe line $-6x-9y=0$ has slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y$-intercept $0$, different from $-5$. Therefore, it is a distinct parallel line.\n\n$$\n-6x-9y=0\n$$",
